        "## Data Cleaning"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "After inspecting the data we applied systematic preprocessing inorder to resolve inconsistencies by stripping whitespace and normalizing categorical labels (e.g., EmploymentStatus, EducationLevel, LoanApproved) and converted monetary columns from strings to numeric values.Also, convertion of LoanDuration to an integer and removed duplicate rows and non-informative identifiers (ApplicationNumber) as it is an identifier, not a predictive featur and removing it prevents noise, overfitting, and misleading importance scores, and dropped BankruptcyHistory when it contained a single valueHere because a constant column has the same value for all rows, meaning it provides no variance, no information gain, and cannot help distinguish between approved and unapproved applicants. Since it adds no predictive value and only increases dataset size, a constant feature like *BankruptcyHistory* is removed.\n",
        "Additionally, missing values were handled in which numeric values were filled with the median as it is robust to outliers and categorical values with the mode, and eliminating null values rom the dataset as shown in the “Missing values after cleaning” output.Then, feuture engineering to the Monthly_Debt_To_Income_Ratio (MonthlyLoanPayment ÷ MonthlyIncome) to capture each applicant’s financial leverage, ensuring division-by-zero was handled to keep the values meaningful. Finally, categorical variables were transformed using one-hot encoding so the model receives numeric inputs without creating false order between categories; drop_first=True prevents multicollinearity by removing redundant dummy columns. The resulting dataset shape (10957 × 28) and class distribution (~76% not approved, 24% approved) were reported to guide model selection and evaluation."
      ],
